    ("Transactions", "What is 'optimistic concurrency control', and how does it typically detect conflicts?", "It always locks rows immediately before any read", "It allows transactions to proceed without locking, then checks (often via a version number or timestamp) at commit time whether the data changed underneath it", "It disables all concurrency entirely", "It is a synonym for pessimistic locking", "B", "Optimistic concurrency assumes conflicts are rare, letting transactions read and compute freely, then validates at commit time - often by comparing a row version - and aborts/retries if a conflict is detected."),
