    ("Normalization", "A denormalized reporting table pre-joins and stores order + customer + product details together. What maintenance cost does this design typically introduce?", "None; denormalized tables never need updates", "Any change to the source customer or product data must also be propagated into every denormalized row that copied it, or the report becomes stale", "It becomes impossible to query the table", "It automatically normalizes itself over time", "B", "Because the same source values are duplicated across many denormalized rows, keeping that copy in sync with the original data requires deliberate update logic (or accepting staleness)."),
    ("Transactions", "What does a 'savepoint' allow within a single transaction?", "Committing part of a transaction permanently while the rest continues", "Rolling back to an intermediate point within the transaction without discarding everything since it began", "Running two transactions simultaneously as one", "Skipping the need for a final COMMIT", "B", "A savepoint marks a point inside a transaction that you can roll back to, undoing only the work done after it while keeping earlier work in that same transaction intact."),
    ("Subqueries", "Why can rewriting a correlated subquery as an equivalent JOIN sometimes significantly improve performance?", "JOINs are always executed in constant time", "A JOIN lets the optimizer consider set-based execution plans (like hash or merge joins) instead of conceptually looping per outer row", "Subqueries are not valid SQL and must always be rewritten", "JOINs never require reading the same table twice", "B", "Rewriting a correlated subquery as a JOIN often lets the query planner use efficient set-based join algorithms, rather than a per-row nested-loop pattern implied by some correlated subqueries."),
